# ⌚ Patek Philippe Movement TPD & Winding Direction Guide

Maintaining a **Patek Philippe** requires a deep understanding of its horological soul. Unlike many standard movements, Patek's calibers—especially the legendary ultra-thin **Calibre 240** and the **Calibre 324/26-330**—have specific winding requirements to maintain their exquisite complications.

## 🛠 The Precision Challenge
Patek Philippe watches often feature intricate complications (Perpetual Calendars, Minute Repeaters). Using an incorrect winding direction or a winder with magnetic interference can disrupt the delicate balance of these masterworks.

## 📊 Patek Philippe TPD Data Table (Core Calibers)

| Patek Philippe Series | Movement (Calibre) | TPD Setting | Direction |
| :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- |
| **Nautilus / Aquanaut** | Cal. 324 S C / 26-330 | 650 - 800 | Clockwise (CW) |
| **Calatrava (Micro-rotor)** | Cal. 240 | 650 - 800 | Counter-Clockwise (CCW) |
| **Grand Complications** | Cal. R 27 / CH 29 | 800 | Both / CW (Varies) |
| **Annual Calendar** | Cal. 324 S QA | 800 | Clockwise (CW) |
| **Twenty~4 Automatic** | Cal. 324 S C | 650 | Clockwise (CW) |

> **⚠️ Critical Note:** Unlike Rolex, many Patek Philippe calibers are **Uni-directional** (Single direction). Setting your winder to "Both" may result in the watch not being wound at all.

